    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Berber dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> origin that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunisia">Tunisia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a> between 1229 and 1574.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a> prospered on grain, wool and leather exports and on treaties that let Italian and Catalan merchants trade under their own consuls.</li><li>Squeezed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in the sixteenth century, the dynasty fell when Ottoman forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a> in 1574.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1257 an embassy from the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahara">Sahara</a> and presented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-i-al-mustansir">al-Mustansir</a> with a giraffe, which caused a sensation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

