    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ibn-tumart"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart.jpg" alt="Ibn Tumart" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spanish_engraving_depicting_Ibn_Tumart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joan Serra y Pausas, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hafsid-dynasty">Hafsids</a> took their name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-hafs-umar-al-hintati">Abu Hafs Umar al-Hintati</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#masmuda">Masmuda</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#berbers">Berber</a> chief from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a> who was among the closest companions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a>, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> movement, and a leading figure of the early Almohad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">Caliphate</a>. His family remained part of the Almohad ruling elite. In 1207, after the Almohad caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-al-nasir">Muhammad al-Nasir</a> had driven the Banu Ghaniya, Almoravid holdouts who had overrun much of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maghreb">Maghreb</a>, out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a>, he appointed Abu Hafs’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-muhammad-abd-al-wahid-ibn-abi-hafs">Abu Muhammad Abd al-Wahid</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunisia">Tunisia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a>. Abu Muhammad held the province until his death in 1221 and made the governorship a family preserve. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-zakariya-yahya-i">Abu Zakariya Yahya</a> became governor in the late 1220s and in 1229 broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, where the caliph al-Ma’mun had repudiated the doctrine of Ibn Tumart. Presenting himself as the true guardian of Almohad orthodoxy, Abu Zakariya at first styled himself only emir, but he quickly built an independent state. He took Constantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bejaia">Bejaia</a> in 1230 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algiers">Algiers</a> in 1235, and in 1242 forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zayyanid-dynasty">Zayyanid</a> ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, Yaghmurasan, to acknowledge his overlordship. For a time several Andalusi cities and the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marinid-dynasty">Marinid</a> state also offered him nominal allegiance, making Tunis the strongest Muslim capital in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> when he died in 1249.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#tunis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tunis.jpg" alt="Tunis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minaret_et_patio_de_la_mosqu%C3%A9e_Zitouna_au_centre_de_la_M%C3%A9dina_de_Tunis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">T A, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> combined an urban, literate society on the coast with Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#berbers">Berber</a> tribal groups in the steppe and the interior, many descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#banu-sulaym">Banu Sulaym</a> who had entered the region in the eleventh century. The coastal plains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahel">Sahel</a> produced grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#olive-oil">olive oil</a>, and the tribes raised sheep and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#camel">camels</a>; wool, hides and leather were major exports. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a>, with its souks clustered around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-zaytuna-mosque">Zaytuna Mosque</a>, was the economic centre, linked by sea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a>, Catalonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a> and by caravan to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> region: in 1257 an embassy from the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-i-al-mustansir">al-Mustansir</a> a giraffe. Treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pisa">Pisa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genoa">Genoa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#venice">Venice</a>, Sicily and the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> allowed Christian merchants to live in their own funduqs, with consuls and chapels, and customs duties on this trade were a major source of revenue. Coral fishing off the coast attracted Italian and Catalan contractors. Society was overwhelmingly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maliki-school">Maliki</a>, with Jewish communities in Tunis and other towns. Successive waves of refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, especially after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#valencia">Valencia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a> in the 1230s and 1240s, brought administrators, scholars, craftsmen and new styles of building and gardening. In the fifteenth century corsairing from Tunisian ports grew into another source of income.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#eighth-crusade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eighth-crusade.jpg" alt="Eighth Crusade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#eighth-crusade">Eighth Crusade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SmrtLudvika91270.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean Fouquet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid</a> armies drew on several sources. The core was formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> tribal contingents and the sultan’s own guard, which included a well-documented Christian militia of Catalan and Castilian soldiers who served the dynasty for long periods under their own commanders. Arab tribes of the interior supplied cavalry in exchange for grants and tax revenues, but their loyalty depended on payment and they often switched sides in dynastic disputes. Andalusi immigrants also served as soldiers and engineers. The dynasty’s greatest test came in 1270, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carthage">Carthage</a> on what became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eighth-crusade">Eighth Crusade</a>; disease ravaged the camp, Louis died near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a> in August, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> negotiated a treaty in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-i-al-mustansir">al-Mustansir</a> paid an indemnity and renewed tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sicily">Sicily</a>. Sicilian and Aragonese fleets later raided the coast, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a> seized the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#djerba">Jerba</a> in 1284; it was recovered only in 1335. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marinid-dynasty">Marinid</a> sultan Abu al-Hasan occupied Tunis in 1347, but his defeat by Arab tribes near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kairouan">Kairouan</a> in 1348 ended the occupation, and a second Marinid invasion in 1357 was also short-lived. In 1390 a French and Genoese expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-ii-duke-of-bourbon">Louis II, Duke of Bourbon</a>, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahdia">Mahdia</a> without success. Fortified harbours, the kasbah of Tunis and coastal watchtowers formed the main defences.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-i-al-mustansir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-al-mustansir.jpg" alt="Muhammad I al-Mustansir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-i-al-mustansir">Muhammad I al-Mustansir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hafsid_dinar_-_Tunisia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DrFO.Jr.Tn, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid</a> state preserved the forms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>. The ruler was at first an emir, but Abu Zakariya’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-i-al-mustansir">Muhammad I al-Mustansir</a> (r. 1249–1277) took the caliphal title of commander of the faithful in 1253, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-baghdad-1258">Mongol sack of Baghdad</a> in 1258 he was briefly recognised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sharif-of-mecca">Sharif of Mecca</a>. A council of Almohad shaykhs, headed by the shaykh of the Almohads, advised the ruler, and Almohad doctrine was honoured in the Friday sermon even as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maliki-school">Maliki</a> jurists, who had always dominated the courts, regained full influence. The administration was organised in ministries for the army, finance and correspondence, staffed largely by Andalusi secretaries such as the poet and historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-al-abbar">Ibn al-Abbar</a>, who was executed by al-Mustansir in 1260. Provinces were held by princes of the dynasty or by appointed governors, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bejaia">Bejaia</a> and Constantine often acted as rival centres: Bejaia formed a separate Hafsid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emirate">emirate</a> between 1285 and 1309. In the fourteenth century the chamberlain, or <em>hajib</em>, often held real power, most notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-tafrakin">Ibn Tafrakin</a> in the 1340s and 1350s. Strong rulers restored central authority, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-al-abbas-ahmad-ii">Abu al-Abbas Ahmad II</a> (r. 1370–1394), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-faris-abd-al-aziz-ii">Abu Faris Abd al-Aziz II</a> (r. 1394–1434) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-amr-uthman">Abu Amr Uthman</a> (r. 1435–1488), who curbed the tribes, reorganised finances and endowed religious foundations.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#muqaddimah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muqaddimah.jpg" alt="Muqaddimah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#muqaddimah">Muqaddimah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Muqaddima_d%27Ibn_Khaldoun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Riad Salih, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hafsid-dynasty">Hafsids</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a> became one of the main intellectual centres of the Islamic west. Abu Zakariya founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madrasa-al-shammaiyya">Shamma’iyya</a>, generally regarded as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madrasa">madrasa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, and his successors endowed further colleges, libraries and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-zaytuna-mosque">Zaytuna Mosque</a>, whose teaching circles drew students from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maghreb">Maghreb</a>. The jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-arafa">Ibn Arafa</a> (d. 1401) made Tunis a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maliki-school">Maliki</a> law, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, born in Tunis in 1332 to a family of Andalusi origin, received his education there, served several Hafsid rulers and returned to teach in the city in the 1370s and 1380s before leaving for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo">Cairo</a>, where he completed the work that included his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muqaddimah">Muqaddimah</a></em>. Andalusi refugees gave Tunisian architecture, music and cuisine a lasting Andalusi character. The dynasty’s commercial treaties made Tunis a regular port of call for Italian and Catalan shipping and set a pattern of negotiated coexistence between Christian merchants and a Muslim state that later Tunisian rulers continued. Politically, the Hafsids gave Ifriqiya a stable identity distinct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, centred on Tunis, which survived the Ottoman conquest: the beylical state of the seventeenth to nineteenth centuries governed much the same territory from the same capital, and modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunisia">Tunisia</a> largely follows those frontiers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#oruc-reis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oruc-reis.jpg" alt="Oruç Reis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#oruc-reis">Oruç Reis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arolsen_Klebeband_01_465_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johann Theodor de Bry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid</a> power weakened after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abu-amr-uthman">Abu Amr Uthman</a> in 1488, as succession disputes, tribal revolts and declining revenues reduced the sultans’ hold beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a>. In the early sixteenth century the state was caught between the expanding Spanish monarchy and Ottoman-backed corsairs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bejaia">Bejaia</a> and Tripoli in 1510, while the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oruc-reis">Oruç</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a> operated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#la-goulette">La Goulette</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#djerba">Jerba</a> with Hafsid permission before founding their own state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algiers">Algiers</a>. In 1534 Hayreddin, now an Ottoman admiral, seized Tunis and drove out the sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mulay-hasan">Mulay Hasan</a>. The following year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> led a large expedition that captured La Goulette and Tunis, sacked the city and restored Mulay Hasan as a Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a>, with a Spanish garrison at La Goulette. Mulay Hasan was deposed and blinded by his son Ahmad in the early 1540s, and Hafsid authority shrank to Tunis and its surroundings while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kairouan">Kairouan</a> and other towns went their own way. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uluj-ali">Uluj Ali</a>, the Ottoman governor of Algiers, took Tunis in 1569; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-of-austria">Don John of Austria</a> recaptured it in 1573 and installed a Hafsid prince. In 1574 an Ottoman fleet and army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#koca-sinan-pasha">Sinan Pasha</a> and Uluj Ali took Tunis and La Goulette, the last Hafsid ruler was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#istanbul">Istanbul</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> became Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunisia">Tunisia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Hafsid Sultanate turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> into an independent state centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunis">Tunis</a> that lasted three and a half centuries, prospered through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> commerce and Andalusi talent, produced scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, and fixed the political shape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunisia">Tunisia</a> before falling between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</em></p>

